# {{name}}

A web app built with [Ironhold](https://github.com/ironhold-rs/ironhold): secure by default, with sign up, log in and log out ready to go.

## Run it

```bash
ironhold dev
```

This builds the app, starts it at <http://127.0.0.1:3000>, and rebuilds and restarts it whenever you save a file. If a change doesn't compile, the last working version keeps running. You can also use `cargo run`.

{{database_notes}}

## Test

```bash
cargo test
```

Tests drive the app like a browser with `ironhold::testing::TestClient`.

## Project layout

| Path | What's in it |
|---|---|
| `src/lib.rs` | Routes |
| `src/auth.rs` | Sign up, log in, log out |
| `src/pages.rs` | Pages |
| `src/user.rs` | Users and their queries |
| `src/layout.rs` | The shared page layout |
| `migrations/` | Database migrations, applied on startup |
| `tests/` | Tests |

## Configuration

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `DATABASE_URL` | Where the database is |
| `IRONHOLD_ENV` | `development` or `production` |
| `IRONHOLD_ADDR` | Listen address (default `127.0.0.1:3000`) |
| `IRONHOLD_TRUSTED_PROXIES` | Addresses of reverse proxies in front of the app, e.g. `127.0.0.1` |
| `RUST_LOG` | Log level, e.g. `info` or `debug` |

`ironhold dev` reads these from `.env` (see `.env.example`).

## Deploy

```bash
cargo build --release
```

That produces one binary, `target/release/{{name}}`, with migrations built in. Run it with `IRONHOLD_ENV=production` and `IRONHOLD_ADDR=0.0.0.0:8080` behind an HTTPS proxy. HTTPS is required in production: session cookies are only sent over HTTPS.

Use a proxy that reads the whole request body before passing it on, such as Nginx (it does by default). Caddy streams bodies through, so slow uploads reach the app. Then tell the app where the proxy connects from, so the login throttle and upload limits see each visitor's address instead of the proxy's: `IRONHOLD_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1` when it runs on the same machine.
